<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Greenville</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1795</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northwest-indian-war">Northwest Indian War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Greenville was signed on 3 August 1795 at Fort Greenville, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the nations of the Western Confederacy after their defeat at the Battle of Fallen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">Timbers</a>. In return for payments in goods, the Native nations ceded most of present-day Ohio and parts of Indiana, establishing the Greenville Treaty Line and ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northwest-indian-war">Northwest Indian War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-greenville.jpg" alt="Treaty of Greenville" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_Greenville_page1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
